refentry.refsect1:
&reftitle.description;
boolpcntl_async_signals boolnullenable&null;
If the enable parameter is &null;, pcntl_async_signals returns whether asynchronous signal handling is enabled. Otherwise, asynchronous signal handling is enabled or disabled.

&reftitle.parameters;


  enable



    Whether asynchronous signal handling should be enabled.

  &reftitle.examples;

   Calling signal handlers during a cURL transfer

    Without the progress callback, the handler would not be called until the
    transfer has completed.



<?php
pcntl_async_signals(true);

pcntl_signal(SIGALRM, function () {
    echo "Heartbeat\n";
    pcntl_alarm(1);
});
pcntl_alarm(1);

$ch = curl_init('https://example.com/slow-endpoint');
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true);

// Calling back into PHP code during the transfer lets the handler run
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_NOPROGRESS, false);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION, function () {
    return 0;
});

$response = curl_exec($ch);
?>
